// @test: run
// @tier: 0
// @level: L0
// @tags: vbc, e2e, interp, thread_local, static, ctors, T0732
// @timeout: 30000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout: 9\n7\n12
//
// ONE directive with escaped newlines, not three directives. Written as
// three `// @expected-stdout:` lines the runner keeps only the LAST and
// compares the whole output against `12`, reporting a mismatch for a
// program that printed exactly the right thing. Spec 627 in this same
// directory is the precedent: nine values on one line.
//
// A static's INITIALISER has run by the time `main` reads it.
//
// This spec exists because the suite could not see the defect it pins.
// `phase_interpret_for_test` — the entry point vtest drives — did not
// call `run_global_ctors`, which `phase_interpret` (the `verum run`
// path) and `run_compiled_vbc` (the cached-VBC path) both do. The
// omission was deliberate and its comment said why:
//
//     // Step 2b: Skip global constructors in test mode.
//     // Global ctors are primarily FFI library initializers (e.g.
//     // kernel32.dll) that fail on macOS and corrupt interpreter
//     // state. VBC interpreter tests don't need FFI initialization.
//
// The premise had expired. Ctors are also `@thread_local` static
// initialisers and the CBGR allocator's LOCAL_HEAP/CURRENT_HEAP
// bootstrap; without them a static reads `Value`'s `default()` out of a
// slot nothing wrote. Measured 2026-09-03 on one file, one binary:
//
//     verum run   ->  9
//     the harness ->  ()      Stdout mismatch, Expected 9
//
// WHY NO EXISTING SPEC CAUGHT IT. `test_952_thread_local.vr` covers the
// same feature and is `@test: unit` — it asserts no output, so a static
// reading as `()` passes it. A feature can be well covered and still
// have the ONE property nobody asserts: that the value arrives.
//
// The three cases below are one each of the shapes that reach a static
// differently, because the initialiser running is not the same fact as
// the read finding it:
//   READ       directly in `main`
//   THROUGH A CALL, so the read happens in another frame
//   AFTER A WRITE, so the slot is real storage and not a re-evaluated
//                  initialiser — the distinction the skip-comment above
//                  turned on, and the reason a `static mut` cannot take
//                  the constant-function path.

@thread_local static READ_DIRECTLY: Int = 9;
@thread_local static READ_THROUGH_CALL: Int = 7;
static mut WRITTEN_THEN_READ: Int = 11;

fn read_it() -> Int {
    READ_THROUGH_CALL
}

fn main() {
    print(READ_DIRECTLY);
    print(read_it());
    WRITTEN_THEN_READ = 12;
    print(WRITTEN_THEN_READ);
}
